  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle": [
      null,
      "## Definition\n\nLet $M$ be a finite-dimensional smooth manifold, let $d\\geq1$ be an integer, and give $\\mathbb C^d$ its standard Hermitian inner product. Let $P:M\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ map such that $P(x)^2=P(x)=P(x)^*$ and $\\operatorname{rank}P(x)=r$ is constant. Define\n$$E_P=\\{(x,v)\\in M\\times\\mathbb C^d:P(x)v=v\\},\\qquad \\pi(x,v)=x.$$\nIts fiber at $x$ is $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$ with the inner product inherited from $\\mathbb C^d$. For a $C^1$ section $s:U\\to\\mathbb C^d$ over an open $U\\subseteq M$ satisfying $P s=s$, define the projected connection by $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$, where $d$ is the componentwise exterior derivative. For a $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, put $P_\\gamma(t)=P(\\gamma(t))$ and $K_\\gamma(t)=[P_\\gamma'(t),P_\\gamma(t)]$. Kato transport along $\\gamma$ is the matrix solution $W_\\gamma'(t)=K_\\gamma(t)W_\\gamma(t)$, $W_\\gamma(0)=I_d$.\n\nThe connection and Kato transport are defined from the projector family itself; no Schrödinger Hamiltonian or physical time evolution is needed. A coherent uniformly gapped spectral projection of a $C^2$ finite Hermitian family is one source of such a $C^2$ family: under Countable Choice, an earlier finite-dimensional adiabatic result supplies that regularity."
    ],
    "lem-nrqmx-projector-bundle-kato-well-definedness": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], and write $E_P$ for its range family. Then $E_P\\to M$ is a rank-$r$ $C^2$ complex vector bundle, and $\\nabla s=P\\,ds$ is a Hermitian connection on it. For every $C^2$ path $\\gamma:[0,1]\\to M$, the Kato equation $W'=[P_\\gamma',P_\\gamma]W$, $W(0)=I_d$, has a unique $C^2$ solution. That solution is unitary and satisfies $P_\\gamma(t)W(t)=W(t)P_\\gamma(0)$ for every $t\\in[0,1]$."
    ],
    "lem-nrqmx-c1-stokes-compact-surface": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth surface with boundary, and let $D\\subseteq M$ be a compact oriented region whose boundary consists of finitely many $C^2$ arcs meeting at finitely many corners. Let $\\eta=a\\,dx+b\\,dy$ be a complex-valued $C^1$ one-form on a neighborhood of $D$ in each local chart, where $C^1$ means its coefficients and first partial derivatives are continuous up to the boundary. Define $d\\eta=(\\partial_xb-\\partial_ya)\\,dx\\wedge dy$ in each chart; the chain rule and symmetry of second derivatives make this expression coordinate-independent. Give $\\partial D$ the outward-normal-first orientation. Then\n$$\\int_D d\\eta=\\int_{\\partial D}\\eta.$$"
    ],
    "thm-nrqmx-berry-projector-curvature": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $M$, $d$, and $P$ satisfy [[def-nrqmx-smooth-projector-bundle]], with constant rank $r$. The curvature of the projected connection $\\nabla=P\\,d$, as an endomorphism-valued two-form on $\\mathbb C^d$, is\n$$F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P.$$\nFor any local $C^2$ orthonormal frame $V:U\\to\\mathbb C^{d\\times r}$ with $V^*V=I_r$ and $P=VV^*$, its connection matrix $A=V^*dV$ is anti-Hermitian and its curvature matrix is $F_{\\mathrm{fr}}=dA+A\\wedge A=V^*FV$. If $V'=Vg$ for a $C^2$ map $g:U\\to U(r)$, then $A'=g^{-1}Ag+g^{-1}dg$ and $F'_{\\mathrm{fr}}=g^{-1}F_{\\mathrm{fr}}g$. Along every $C^2$ path, Kato transport is unitary and intertwines the endpoint projectors."
    ],
    "lem-nrqmx-c2-projector-chern-integrality": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, let $d\\geq1$, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ family of rank-one orthogonal projections. Let $E_P\\to\\Sigma$ be the complex line bundle with fibers $\\operatorname{ran}P(x)$, let $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and let $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$ be the curvature of this projected connection. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nHere $c_1(E_P)$ is the first integral Chern class of the underlying topological complex line bundle, and $[\\Sigma]$ is the fundamental class for the stated orientation."
    ],
    "thm-nrqmx-adiabatic-geometric-phase-bound": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $d\\geq1$, let $H\\in C^2([0,1],\\operatorname{Herm}(\\mathbb C^d))$ be a finite Hermitian family with energy unit joules, and let $\\hbar>0$ have unit joule-seconds. A spectral cluster at $s$ is a subset $\\sigma_P(s)$ of the finite spectrum of $H(s)$, and $P(s)$ is the orthogonal projection onto the sum of its eigenspaces. Coherently followed means that $s\\mapsto P(s)$ is continuous in operator norm (equivalently, it agrees locally with the Riesz continuation of its initial cluster). Assume the distance from $\\sigma_P(s)$ to the complementary spectrum is at least a uniform $g>0$ in joules. Let $T>0$ be a total duration in seconds, let $s=t/T$ be dimensionless, and let $U_T(0)=I$ solve\n$$i\\hbar\\,\\partial_sU_T(s)=T H(s)U_T(s).$$\nPut $K(s)=[P'(s),P(s)]$. There is a $C^1$ Hermitian matrix $X(s)$ solving $[H(s),X(s)]=K(s)$. With\n$$C=2\\sup_{s\\in[0,1]}\\|X(s)\\|+\\int_0^1\\bigl(\\|X'(s)\\|+\\|K(s)\\|\\,\\|X(s)\\|\\bigr)\\,ds,$$\nthe leakage satisfies\n$$\\|(I-P(s))U_T(s)P(0)\\|\\leq\\frac{\\hbar C}{T}\\qquad(0\\leq s\\leq1).$$\nThe supplier provides a $C^1$ Hermitian $X$ solving the displayed commutator equation. For that $X$, $C$ has unit joule-inverse, so $\\hbar C/T$ is dimensionless. If the cluster has rank one, with $H(s)P(s)=E(s)P(s)$, define $U_A(0)=I$ by\n$$i\\,\\partial_sU_A(s)=\\left(\\frac{T}{\\hbar}H(s)+iK(s)\\right)U_A(s).$$\nFor Kato transport $W'(s)=K(s)W(s)$, $W(0)=I$, the comparison evolution factors exactly on the initial eigenspace:\n$$U_A(s)P(0)=\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{iT}{\\hbar}\\int_0^sE(u)\\,du\\right)W(s)P(0).$$\nThe leakage estimate is finite-dimensional and conditional on the stated uniform positive gap."
    ],
    "thm-nrqmx-rank-one-chern-holonomy": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $\\Sigma$ be a compact boundaryless oriented smooth surface, and let $P:\\Sigma\\to\\operatorname{End}(\\mathbb C^d)$ be a $C^2$ rank-one orthogonal projection family. Let $E_P$ be its complex line bundle, $\\nabla=P\\,d$, and $F=P(dP\\wedge dP)P$. Then\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\pi}\\int_\\Sigma\\operatorname{tr}F=\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle\\in\\mathbb Z.$$\nFor any $C^2$ closed path $\\gamma$ subdivided into local unit frames $u_j$, with $A_j=u_j^*du_j$ and transition $u_{j+1}=u_jg_j$ for $g_j$ valued in $U(1)$ at the crossing points, the holonomy is the scalar by which parallel transport acts on its one-dimensional initial fiber. This scalar is the product of the local factors $\\exp(-\\int_{\\gamma_j}A_j)$ and the transition factors $g_j^{-1}$. Thus geometric holonomy includes the frame-transition scalars whenever one frame does not cover the path.\n\nIn addition, suppose $\\Sigma=\\Sigma_N\\cup\\Sigma_S$ is a decomposition into compact oriented subsurfaces with disjoint interiors and common finite boundary $\\Gamma=\\partial\\Sigma_N=-\\partial\\Sigma_S$, a finite union of oriented smooth circles. If $E_P$ has supplied $C^2$ unit frames $u_N,u_S$ on these subsurfaces and $u_S=u_Ng$ on $\\Gamma$ for a $C^2$ map $g:\\Gamma\\to U(1)$, then\n$$\\langle c_1(E_P),[\\Sigma]\\rangle=\\operatorname{wind}(g):=\\sum_{\\Gamma_j\\subset\\Gamma}\\frac{1}{2\\pi i}\\int_{\\Gamma_j}g^{-1}dg\\in\\mathbb Z,$$\nwhere each component $\\Gamma_j$ has the orientation induced by $\\partial\\Sigma_N$."
    ],
    "lem-nrqmx-spin-half-solid-angle-period": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nAssume full AC. Let $S^2$ be the unit sphere with outward area form $\\omega$, and set\n$$\\sigma_x=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\\sigma_z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad P_+(n)=\\frac{I+n_x\\sigma_x+n_y\\sigma_y+n_z\\sigma_z}{2}.$$\nFor any two $C^2$ maps $f_0,f_1:D^2\\to S^2$ from an oriented disk, with the same boundary map and its induced boundary orientation, the two oriented solid-angle integrals differ by a multiple of $4\\pi$:\n$$\\int_{D^2}f_0^*\\omega-\\int_{D^2}f_1^*\\omega\\in4\\pi\\mathbb Z.$$"
    ],
    "ex-nrqmx-spin-half-monopole": [
